/**
 * Normalizes Contact.Phone values so the dialer only sees digits and a
 * leading plus sign, e.g. "+44 (20) 7946-0018" -> "+442079460018".
 */
public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {
    /** Matches any character that is not a digit and not a plus sign. */
    private static final Pattern NON_DIALABLE = Pattern.compile('[^0-9+]');

    /**
     * Normalizes the phone number of every contact in contactIds that has a
     * phone. All changed contacts are saved in a single partial-success DML
     * call, so one bad record cannot block the others.
     *
     * @param  contactIds contacts to process (up to 200 per the caller)
     * @return map of contact Id -> first error message for every record that
     *         could not be saved; empty when all saves succeeded
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // One query for the whole batch (bulk-safe); user mode enforces CRUD/FLS.
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
            WITH USER_MODE
        ];

        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            // "Has a phone": skip null/blank so we never clear a number by accident.
            if (String.isBlank(c.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }
            c.Phone = NON_DIALABLE.matcher(c.Phone).replaceAll('');
            toUpdate.add(c);
        }

        if (toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // Single partial-success DML; results are returned in list order.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results =
            Database.update(toUpdate, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                failures.put(toUpdate[i].Id, sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage());
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

Your core approach is sound and I kept it: one SOQL for the batch, one `Database.update(..., false)` after the loop, and results zipped back to the input order via index. Results from `Database.update(list, false)` are guaranteed to come back in the same order as the list, so `results[i]` maps to `toUpdate[i].Id`. I also kept "update every contact that has a phone" rather than only the ones whose value changed, per your spec.

Things I changed or added, and why:

1. **`with sharing` on the class.** Apex runs in system mode by default, which ignores sharing rules. Declaring `with sharing` makes the query and DML respect the running user's record access. This is the safe default for a new class.

2. **`WITH USER_MODE` on the query and `AccessLevel.USER_MODE` on the update.** This enforces object/field-level security (CRUD/FLS) in addition to sharing. Without it, a cleanup utility can read and write `Phone` even for users who lack permission. Using `AccessLevel.USER_MODE` also means permission failures come back as ordinary `SaveResult` errors and land in the returned map instead of throwing — which matches your partial-success contract. If you specifically intend this to run in a system-context batch/admin flow, you can drop both and keep plain `Database.update(toUpdate, false)`, but for a user-invoked utility I'd leave the enforcement on. (Note: if the caller lacks *read* access to `Phone`, the `WITH USER_MODE` query throws a `QueryException` rather than returning a map — that's a caller-setup error, not a per-record data error.)

3. **Null/empty guard on `contactIds`.** `WHERE Id IN :contactIds` with a null list throws; an empty list returns nothing. The early return makes the empty-map contract explicit and cheap.

4. **`String.isBlank` for "has a phone".** Treating `null` *and* blank/whitespace as "no phone" avoids turning a whitespace-only value into `''` and wiping it. If you truly want any non-null value normalized (so `"   "` becomes `''`), swap this to `c.Phone == null`.

5. **Precompiled `Pattern` instead of `String.replaceAll`.** `replaceAll` recompiles the regex on every call; a `static final Pattern` compiles once. Negligible at 200 records, but it's the right habit for a method that may be reused.

6. **Single `getErrors()[0]` is safe.** A failed `SaveResult` always has at least one error, so no null/empty guard is needed there.

One deliberate non-change worth flagging: since you update *every* contact with a phone, a contact whose number is already clean still gets a DML call, which re-fires validation rules, flows, and triggers. If that becomes a problem, filter with `if (normalized != c.Phone)` before adding to `toUpdate` — the returned failure map would then only cover records you actually attempted, which is usually what you want. I left it out to honor your "save them all" instruction.

