/**
 * Creates a follow-up Task for each Opportunity passed in.
 *
 * Bulk-safe: a single SOQL query and a single DML statement (Database.insert with
 * allOrNone = false) are used regardless of how many Opportunities are supplied.
 * A Task rejected by a validation rule (or any other record-level failure) does not
 * prevent the remaining Tasks from being created.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /** Number of days after today that the follow-up is due. */
    @TestVisible
    private static final Integer FOLLOW_UP_OFFSET_DAYS = 7;

    /**
     * Creates one follow-up Task per Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Opportunities to create follow-ups for.
     * @return The Ids of the Opportunities for which no Task could be created
     *         (empty when every Task was created successfully).
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate so a repeated Id cannot create duplicate Tasks.
        Set<Id> uniqueOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }

        if (uniqueOpportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Single query for the whole batch.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds
        ]);

        Date activityDate = Date.today().addDays(FOLLOW_UP_OFFSET_DAYS);

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        // Parallel list so a SaveResult can be mapped back to its Opportunity.
        List<Id> taskOpportunityIds = new List<Id>();

        for (Id opportunityId : uniqueOpportunityIds) {
            Opportunity opp = opportunitiesById.get(opportunityId);
            if (opp == null) {
                // Opportunity does not exist or is not visible to the running user.
                failedOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
                continue;
            }

            tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = activityDate,
                Status = 'Not Started',
                Priority = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskOpportunityIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // One DML statement for the entire batch. allOrNone = false gives the same
        // per-record isolation as an individual try/catch insert, but stays well
        // within the 150 DML-statement governor limit.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasksToInsert, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(taskOpportunityIds[i]);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

## What I did differently, and why

I did **not** loop and `insert` each `Task` on its own inside a `try/catch`, and that is the one intentional deviation.

- **The 150 DML-statement governor limit.** A synchronous transaction allows at most 150 DML statements. With the stated 150–250 opportunities, the literal per-record loop issues up to 250 `insert` statements and blows the limit partway through. `System.LimitException` is not a catchable/handleable exception, so the loop's `try/catch` would not save it — the entire transaction would fail and no tasks (or an inconsistent subset) would be committed. This directly violates the requirement that one bad task must not stop the others.
- **`Database.insert(tasks, false)` gives the same isolation, bulk-safely.** `allOrNone = false` commits every record that passes and rejects only the failing ones, returning a `Database.SaveResult` per record. Inspecting `isSuccess()` is the bulk equivalent of the per-record `try/catch`, using one DML statement instead of up to 250.
- **Mapping failures back to opportunities.** I keep a parallel `List<Id> taskOpportunityIds` so index `i` of the `SaveResult` list maps to the opportunity that produced that task. Failed ones go into the returned `Set<Id>`.
- **One SOQL query** for all opportunities (also well within limits), plus guards for `null`/empty input and for an opportunity that doesn't exist or isn't visible (counted as failed, since no task could be created).
- **De-duplication** of the incoming Ids so a repeated Id cannot create duplicate tasks.
- **`with sharing`** so the service respects the running user's record access; the class sharing model is declared explicitly rather than relying on the default. If this service is meant to run in system context (e.g. from a trigger or scheduled job), change it to `without sharing`.

If the per-record `try/catch` style is truly required, the only correct way to keep it is to chunk the work so each transaction stays under 150 DML statements (e.g. batches of ≤100 via `Database.executeBatch`), but `Database.insert(..., false)` is the simpler, standard bulk pattern and is what I'd ship.

Files created. Here is the solution, with the reasoning behind the one change I made to your approach.

## What I changed and why

**I did not load all Contacts (`[SELECT ... FROM Contact]`) into a map.** That query is not viable in this org:

- **SOQL row limit:** synchronous Apex can retrieve 50,000 rows per transaction. With ~2.3M contacts, the query throws `System.LimitException: Too many query rows: 50001` on essentially every account update.
- **Heap limit:** 6 MB synchronous / 12 MB asynchronous. Even a compact map of millions of `Contact` rows blows the heap long before the row limit.
- **It's a full-table scan** on every MDM batch of 200 accounts, and it pulls in the vast majority of contacts that will never be updated (they belong to unchanged accounts or have another country).

Instead I keep the "one query, no queries in loops" contract, but scope it to only the accounts that actually changed: `WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds` (`AccountId` is indexed, so it's selective). Contacts of unchanged accounts, and contacts with a different country, are simply not returned — which is exactly the "leave every other contact alone" rule, enforced twice (by the query and by the in-memory check).

**Kept:** bulk change detection via `Trigger.oldMap`, blank-or-previous-country matching, a single bulk DML, no SOQL/DML in loops.

**Two limits worth knowing for later:** the DML row limit is ~10,000 records per transaction (not per statement, so chunking doesn't get around it) and SOQL is 50,000 rows. If an account ever holds tens of thousands of contacts, a single trigger transaction can't finish the work — move the sync into a chained `Queueable`/`Batchable` that re-queries by `AccountId`. For MDM batches of 200 accounts this is not normally a concern.

**Sharing:** the class is `with sharing` so a user who can edit an Account can't indirectly modify Contacts they can't see. Your MDM integration user (View All Data / Modify All Data) still sees and updates every relevant contact, so the sync stays complete. If your integration user does **not** have broad contact access, change the class to `without sharing` (triggers are system-context anyway) so the sync doesn't silently skip contacts.

**Assumptions:** if the new `BillingCountry` is blank, contacts are set to blank as written ("set MailingCountry to the new BillingCountry"). Case-only changes to `BillingCountry` are treated as no change (Apex `==` on strings is case-insensitive); normal picklist values make this a non-issue.

